+# Paper-Window Real-Data Workflow
+
+The `Paper Window Real Data Replication` workflow is a manual-only GitHub Actions workflow for running the closest available public-data reconstruction of the AGENTICAITA paper window.
+
+## Default reconstruction target
+
+The workflow defaults to the paper-window values used by the repository downloader:
+
+- window: `2026-04-06T00:00:00Z` through `2026-04-11T23:59:59Z`;
+- timeframe: `1m`;
+- profile: `baseline-15`;
+- baseline symbol count: `15`.
+
+## Data-source modes
+
+| Mode | Behaviour |
+| --- | --- |
+| `closest` | Attempts Hyperliquid first, then falls back to Binance USD-M if Hyperliquid does not produce complete exportable OHLCV coverage. |
+| `hyperliquid` | Attempts only Hyperliquid. This is closest to the paper venue, but the documented paper-window attempt may return funding rows without usable OHLCV coverage. |
+| `binanceusdm` | Uses Binance USD-M perpetual futures for the same baseline asset universe as a public comparable-market fallback. |
+
+## What the workflow runs
+
+For the selected venue, the workflow runs:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/run_real_data_replication.py \
+  --profile baseline-15 \
+  --exchange <chosen-exchange> \
+  --symbol-limit 15 \
+  --start 2026-04-06T00:00:00Z \
+  --end 2026-04-11T23:59:59Z
+```
+
+It then runs `scripts/check_replication_quality.py`, builds run manifests, creates a local results index, renders the local HTML dashboard, writes a GitHub Actions job summary, and uploads a compact artefact bundle.
+
+## Uploaded artefacts
+
+The workflow uploads summary and diagnostic artefacts such as coverage reports, run manifests, quality reports, replication summaries, result indexes, and the dashboard. It does not upload raw SQLite databases or per-symbol raw OHLCV CSV directories by default.
+
+The exported `replication_input_ohlcv.csv` can be uploaded by setting `upload_replication_input` to `true` when manually dispatching the workflow.
+
+## Interpretation
+
+This workflow is not an exact reproduction of the paper run. Public APIs can reconstruct comparable market-condition inputs, but they do not recover the original L2 order book snapshots, prompts, LLM calls, agent deliberations, risk-manager approvals, SQLite dry-run records, or execution assumptions.
